Mica reads its settings from environment variables. Every name starts with `MICA_`, except `PORT` and `HOST`, which hosting platforms already use. A blank value counts as not set. `NODE_ENV` changes nothing in Mica.

Plain HTTP is allowed only for an address on this machine (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]`), as in a trial or a test. Everything else needs `https://`. A duration has its unit in the name, in whole units: `_HOURS`, `_MINUTES` or `_SECONDS`. A secret can come from a file, such as a Docker or Kubernetes secret: set the variable with `_FILE` at the end of its name to the file's path.

The self-hosted image reads the environment and a `.env` file in its working directory. The compose file passes the `.env` beside it. The local editor reads the environment only: a site's `.env` holds the site's settings, not the editor's. Its flags win over the environment.

At the start, the image logs a warning for each `MICA_` name that it does not know. That is usually a typo.

## Self-hosted image: required

- `MICA_PUBLIC_URL`: the address that the browser shows, an origin with `https://` and no path, such as `https://cms.example.com`. For a trial on this machine: `http://localhost:6422`. It sets the cookie scope, the Origin check and the OIDC redirect.
- `MICA_SESSION_SECRET`: at least 32 characters, for example from `openssl rand -base64 48`. It encrypts the session cookie, so a new value signs everybody out. Or `MICA_SESSION_SECRET_FILE`: a file that holds it.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_URL`: the HTTPS clone URL of the content repository, without credentials.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_TOKEN`: the token for Git over HTTPS. GitHub: a fine-grained token with Contents: read and write. GitLab: a project access token with `read_repository` and `write_repository`. Or `MICA_REPOSITORY_TOKEN_FILE`: a file that holds it. Mica reads the file for each Git call, so a short-lived token, such as a GitHub App token, can change without a restart.
- `MICA_OIDC_ISSUER` and `MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_ID`: the OpenID Connect provider and the client that you registered there.
- At least one of `MICA_ADMIT_GROUPS`, `MICA_ADMIT_DOMAINS`, `MICA_ADMIT_EMAILS` and `MICA_ADMIT_SUBJECTS`. Mica does not start without a rule that says who may edit.

## Self-hosted image: the repository

- `MICA_REPOSITORY_BRANCH`: the branch that Mica reads and publishes to. Default: `main`.
- `MICA_CONTENT_PATH`: the content root in the repository, such as `content` or `site/content`. Mica writes only below it. Without it, Mica uses the one directory in the repository with a `.mica/schema.json`. Set it only when the repository has more than one. The top of the repository cannot be the content root.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_USERNAME`: the Git user name for the token. Default: `x-access-token` on GitHub, `oauth2` on GitLab, `mica` on other hosts.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_LFS_URL`: the Git LFS address, only when the host does not serve it at the clone URL with `/info/lfs`. Mica also gives the token to this host. Default: the clone URL with `/info/lfs`.
- `MICA_REPOSITORY_SYNC_SECONDS`: how often Mica fetches the branch, from 5 to 86400. Default: `60`.
- `MICA_DATA_DIR`: where Mica keeps its clone, in the folder `repository`. Default: `./data`. The image sets `/data`, the volume.

## Self-hosted image: sign-in and sessions

- `MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET`: the client secret. Leave it out for a public client with PKCE only. Or `MICA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE`: a file that holds it.
- `MICA_OIDC_SCOPE`: the scopes that Mica asks for. Default: `openid profile email`.
- `MICA_OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM`: the claim that holds the person's groups. Default: `groups`.
- `MICA_ADMIT_GROUPS`: groups whose members may edit, separated by commas.
- `MICA_ADMIT_DOMAINS`: email domains whose verified addresses may edit, separated by commas.
- `MICA_ADMIT_EMAILS`: verified email addresses that may edit, separated by commas.
- `MICA_ADMIT_SUBJECTS`: `issuer|subject` pairs that may edit, separated by commas.
- `MICA_SESSION_TTL_HOURS`: the longest session, in hours. Default: `8`.
- `MICA_SESSION_IDLE_HOURS`: a session without a request ends after this many hours. Default: `4`.
- `MICA_SIGNIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS`: sign-in starts from one address in the window. Default: `10`.
- `MICA_SIGNIN_WINDOW_MINUTES`: the window for that limit, in minutes. Default: `15`.

## Self-hosted image: the server

- `HOST`: the address to listen on. Default in the image: `0.0.0.0`.
- `PORT`: the port to listen on. Default: `6422`.
- `MICA_TRUST_PROXY`: read the client address from `X-Forwarded-For`. Set it to `true` only behind a proxy that you control. Default: `false`.
- `MICA_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES`: the largest upload, in bytes. Default: `26214400` (25 MB).
- `MICA_LOG_LEVEL`: `debug`, `info`, `warn`, `error` or `silent`. Default: `info`.
- `MICA_SITE_URL`: the website's address, such as `https://example.com`. With a `url` in the model, an entry shows "Open on website". Added in 0.11.0.

## Self-hosted image: agents

Added in 0.10.0. See [Edit with an agent](/docs/agents/).

- `MICA_MCP`: `true` lets agents connect at `/mcp`. Each person allows each agent on a consent page, after they sign in. Default: `false`.
- `MICA_MCP_PUBLISH`: `true` lets a person also allow an agent to publish. Default: `false`: agents save drafts, and people publish them.

## Local editor

`npx @micacms/editor start [content directory]` takes these flags:

- `--content`, `-c`: the content root. Default: `MICA_CONTENT_ROOT`, else the directory with a `.mica/schema.json` (at most three levels down; not in `node_modules` or a hidden directory), else `./content`. With more than one such directory, name the one to use.
- `--port`, `-p`: the port. Default: `PORT`, else `6422`.
- `--host`: the address to listen on. Only a loopback address is allowed. Default: `HOST`, else `127.0.0.1`.
- `--no-open`: do not open the browser.

It also reads these variables:

- `MICA_CONTENT_ROOT`: the content root, when no path is given.
- `MICA_PUBLIC_URL`: the editor's own address. It must be on this computer. Default: `http://localhost:` and the port.
- `MICA_LOCAL_PUBLISH`: `false` shows the pending changes, but Publish does not commit or push. Default: `true`.
- `MICA_MCP_PUBLISH`: `true` gives agents the `publish` tool, at `/mcp` and in `npx mica mcp`. Default: `false`. `npx mica mcp --allow-publish` does the same.
- `MICA_SITE_URL`, `MICA_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES` and `MICA_LOG_LEVEL`, as for the image.

## For work on Mica itself

The local editor only, not the image:

- `MICA_ADMIN_ORIGINS`: more browser origins that may call the admin API, separated by commas. The Vite dev server of the editor UI needs it.
- `MICA_ADMIN_DIR`: serve the editor UI from another directory.
